In the 1870s, [[@steam-engine|railways]] were spreading across the American West, and diggers found huge bones in Colorado and Wyoming. At Como Bluff in Wyoming, two [[@transcontinental-railroad|railway workers]] wrote to Marsh about bones sticking out of a ridge. It became one of the richest dinosaur sites in the world.

Both men hired collectors who worked in tough, lonely conditions. They spied on each other's camps and lured away each other's workers. Some teams smashed bones they could not carry, rather than leave them for rivals. Some digs took place on land that belonged to [[@native-nations|Native American nations]]. On a trip in 1874, Marsh met the Lakota leader Red Cloud, and later spoke up for him against corrupt government agents.

{{story:newspapers}}

In June 2013, floods and landslides trapped thousands of pilgrims and villagers in the mountains of Uttarakhand. The Air Force launched Operation Rahat, flying helicopters and planes for weeks through rain and cloud to lift people to safety. One helicopter crashed during the rescue, killing everyone on board.

The Air Force also flies after [[cyclone|cyclones]] in Odisha and floods in Kerala and Assam, often working with the army, navy and other [[@disaster-response|disaster]] teams. It has flown aid to Nepal after the 2015 earthquake and evacuated Indians from countries in conflict.

{{story:indian-astronauts}}

A freshly moulted crab is like a knight without armour. Fish, birds, octopuses and even other crabs will eat it. So it hides under rocks, in burrows or among [[@mangroves|mangrove]] roots until its new shell is hard, usually a few days for small crabs and longer for big ones.

People catch crabs at exactly this moment. In the USA, blue crabs from Chesapeake Bay are sold as "soft-shell crabs" and fried whole, shell and all.

{{story:exoskeletons}}

Swim near a healthy coral reef with a hydrophone and you hear a constant sizzling. Most of it comes from [[@power-pistol-shrimp|snapping shrimp]]. Each has one oversized claw that snaps shut so fast it shoots out a jet of water and a tiny collapsing bubble. The pop is loud enough to stun small prey.

During the Second World War, [[@submarine-tech|US Navy submarines]] sometimes hid among these shrimp beds, because the crackle drowned out their own noise. Reef sound also helps young fish find a home. In 2019, scientists working on Australia's Great Barrier Reef played recordings of healthy reefs over damaged ones, and more young fish settled there.

{{story:sound-travel}}

Pure graphite was rare. In 1795, during a war that cut France off from English graphite, a French scientist named Nicolas-Jacques Conté found a solution. He ground poorer graphite into powder, mixed it with clay and water, and baked the paste in a [[kiln|kiln]].

This had a bonus. More clay made a harder, lighter pencil. More graphite made a softer, darker one. That's where the pencil scale comes from. "H" means hard and "B" means black. An HB pencil is in the middle. A 6B is very soft and dark, good for drawing, while a 4H draws pale, thin lines.

{{story:gems-crystals}}

In 1839, a 19-year-old French scientist named Edmond Becquerel was experimenting in his father's lab. He found that shining light on certain materials dipped in a liquid made a tiny electric current. This is now called the [[photovoltaic|photovoltaic]] effect, from the Greek word for light and the name of [[@batteries|Volta]], the battery pioneer.

For over a century, solar cells stayed weak and expensive. Then, in 1954, three scientists at Bell Labs in the USA made a silicon cell that turned about 6% of sunlight into electricity, enough to power a small radio. Newspapers called it the start of a new age. Even so, the first panels cost so much that only governments and big companies could afford them. Prices have since fallen so far that, in many sunny countries, solar is now among the cheapest ways to make electricity.

{{story:satellites-help}}

Painting on a wall without permission is against the law in most places, so a street artist has good reason to stay hidden. Over time the secret also became part of the art. People talk about who Banksy might be almost as much as they talk about the pictures.

Some reporters have named people they believe are Banksy. None has been confirmed. Banksy speaks through a website and an office called Pest Control, which decides which works are real. That keeps the person and the art apart, a bit like a writer using a pen name. For an artist whose work questions power, [[@privacy|privacy]] is also a kind of protection.

{{story:free-speech}}

The secret of a safe fall is slowing down gently. When you land on something hard, you stop in an instant, and that sudden stop causes injuries. When you land on a giant airbag or a stack of cardboard boxes, you sink in and slow down over a longer time.

That's the same idea behind car [[@airbags|airbags]] and crash mats in gymnastics. For very high falls, performers use special rigs with cables that slow them down just before they hit the bag. From a 10-storey building, a falling person reaches about 80 km/h, so every detail counts.

{{story:airbags}}

Before this, most artists sketched outdoors but painted in their studios. The Impressionists took their easels outside, a practice called painting *en plein air*, French for "in the open air".

Two inventions helped. In 1841 an American painter, John Goffe Rand, invented the squeezable metal paint tube, so artists could carry ready-made colours anywhere. And new railway lines let painters from Paris reach the countryside and seaside in a few hours. [[@trains-how|Trains]] themselves became a favourite subject, puffing clouds of steam into the light.

{{story:photography-history}}

On 15 March 1962, US President John F. Kennedy sent a message to Congress listing four rights for every consumer: the right to safety, the right to be informed, the right to choose and the right to be heard. "Consumers, by definition, include us all," he said.

The date is now marked around the world as World Consumer Rights Day. Over time, campaigners added more rights, such as the right to have a problem fixed and the right to learn about being a smart buyer.

{{story:seatbelts}}

The cooking you see is only the end of a long day. Chefs arrive hours before service to check deliveries of vegetables, meat and spices. Then comes the preparation: chopping onions by the kilo, making stocks and sauces, marinating meat, rolling dough.

French cooks call this mise en place, "everything in its place". By the time the first customer orders, each station has its ingredients ready in small containers. During service, there is no time to stop and chop. After the last order, the kitchen must be scrubbed clean before anyone goes home.

{{story:ratios-cooking}}

Over 2,000 years ago, Greek scholars such as Hipparchus imagined lines wrapped around the globe. Lines of [[latitude|latitude]] run east to west and tell you how far north or south of the [[equator|equator]] you are. Lines of longitude, or [[meridian|meridians]], run from pole to pole and tell you how far east or west you are.

Together they make [[@coordinates|coordinates]]. Delhi is at roughly 28.6° north and 77.2° east, and New York at about 40.7° north and 74.0° west. Latitude has a natural starting line, the equator. Longitude doesn't, so in 1884 a conference in Washington chose the meridian through Greenwich, in London, as zero.

{{story:gps-how}}

On a [[@piano|piano]], each octave is split into 12 steps called semitones, counting the black keys and the white keys. In the tuning most pianos use, called equal temperament, every step multiplies the frequency by the same amount: the twelfth root of 2, which is about 1.0595. Twelve steps multiply it by exactly 2.

This is a compromise. Pure ratios like 3:2 are slightly out with equal steps, but the trade-off lets musicians play in any key. Indian classical music works differently. It traditionally speaks of 22 *shrutis*, fine shades of pitch, and singers often slide between notes rather than jumping.

{{story:ears-hear}}

Much of a hospital's work happens where patients rarely go. In the lab, scientists test blood and other samples under [[@microscopes|microscopes]] and in machines, looking for [[infection|infections]] or checking how organs are working. In the blood bank, donated blood is sorted by [[@blood-groups|blood group]] and stored, ready for operations and accidents.

Radiology uses [[@x-rays|X-rays]], [[ultrasound|ultrasound]] and scanners to look inside the body. The pharmacy prepares medicines. Kitchens cook special meals, and laundries wash huge piles of sheets every day.

{{story:x-rays}}

In 1799 Napoleon returned to France and took power in a coup. Five years later, at Notre-Dame Cathedral in Paris, he took the crown from the Pope's hands and placed it on his own head.

He reorganised France. The Napoleonic Code of 1804 replaced a jumble of local rules with one set of laws. It ended privileges based on birth and protected property, though it gave women fewer rights than men. He set up state secondary schools called lycées, founded the Bank of France and spread use of the [[@metric-system|metric system]]. He also restored slavery in French colonies in 1802, which had been abolished in 1794.

{{story:new-orleans}}

Doctors in the 1800s believed many diseases came from foul air rising from swamps and hot plains. The word malaria itself comes from Italian for "bad air". Hill air felt cleaner, so the hills seemed healthier.

They were partly right, for the wrong reason. Malaria is spread by [[@mosquito-diseases|mosquitoes]], and the mosquitoes that carry it struggle in cold, high places. So soldiers did recover better in the hills. They just didn't know why until Ronald Ross proved the mosquito link in India in 1897.

{{story:tea}}

The [[@mysore-palace|Mysore Palace]] that stands today was built between 1897 and 1912, after an older wooden palace burned down. It was designed by a British architect, Henry Irwin, mixing Indian, Islamic and European styles, with domes, arches and towers.

During Dasara, the palace is outlined with nearly a lakh bulbs. When they switch on in the evening, the whole building seems to be drawn in gold [[@light-bulb|light]]. Musicians perform in the courtyard, and crowds gather on the lawns. For many families, seeing the lit palace is the highlight of the year.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

For four years, Utzon and the engineering firm Ove Arup & Partners tried shape after shape. The shells Utzon had drawn were free curves, each one different. That would have needed thousands of special moulds, and no one could calculate whether they would stand. The team used some of the first computers ever put to work on a building to test the sums.

In about 1961, Utzon found the answer. If every shell was cut from the surface of one sphere, all the curves would match. The shells could be made from ribs of [[@cement-concrete|concrete]] cast in a few repeated moulds on the ground, then lifted by crane and bolted together. A bit of [[@geometry-everyday|geometry]] had saved the whole project.

{{story:geometry-everyday}}

In September 1893, a young Indian monk, [[@swami-vivekananda|Swami Vivekananda]], stood up at the World's Parliament of Religions in Chicago. He began: "Sisters and brothers of America." Before he could go on, the hall broke into applause that lasted a couple of minutes.

Those five words felt warm and equal at a time when many speakers began with stiff formal greetings. He went on to speak about respect between religions. In India, his birthday, 12 January, is now celebrated as National Youth Day.

{{story:voice}}

Apple seeds don't grow "true". Each seed has a mix of [[@dna|genes]] from two parents, so its fruit is usually small and sour, and different from the apple it came from.

To get the same apple every time, growers use grafting: they attach a twig from a good tree onto the roots of another. Every Gala or Fuji apple tree is grown this way. In the early 1800s, an American named John Chapman, known as Johnny Appleseed, planted apple nurseries from seed across Ohio and Indiana. His apples were mostly used to make cider.

{{story:newton-life}}

[[botanist|Botanists]] think tamarind first grew wild in [[tropical|tropical]] Africa, perhaps in the drier grasslands of the east. It reached India so long ago that it now seems completely at home. Old Sanskrit texts mention it, and it has been part of Indian cooking for centuries.

Arab traders bought it in Indian ports and called it *tamar hindi*, "the Indian date", because the pulp looked like dried dates. Europeans heard that name and turned it into "tamarind". Spanish and Portuguese sailors later carried tamarind across the Atlantic. It grows widely in [[@mexico|Mexico]], where children eat tamarind sweets coated in chilli and drink chilled *agua de tamarindo*.

